course: dictionary
lesson: 6679
title: Oblivion and the words around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Oblivion is a noun, a naming word for a state. Its main meaning is being completely forgotten. Look at the clues in this sentence: «with its soft touch came instant oblivion». Instant means straight away, and a soft touch suggests something gentle sending you away. So oblivion here means you lose all awareness at once, like drifting off.
explain: The clue words in a sentence point to the meaning. «The old song fell into oblivion»: fell suggests a drop, and the song is no longer remembered by anyone. Ask: what word would fit here? Try "forgotten" in the sentence. It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: Oblivion can also mean being wiped out completely. If a team is «knocked into oblivion», it is beaten so badly it disappears from the game. The dictionary gives three senses, so read the whole sentence before you decide which one fits.
fact: Oblivion is a noun, a naming word.
fact: Oblivion is uncountable: you say "oblivion", not "oblivions".
fact: «Oblivion» means the state of being completely forgotten.
fact: «Oblivion» can also mean deep sleep, or not noticing what is happening around you.
fact: «Oblivion» can mean being destroyed or wiped out completely.
exercise: «A breeze blows lightly and caressingly in their faces; and with its soft touch came instant oblivion.» What does oblivion mean here? | Loud singing | A strong wind that knocks people over | A bright light in the sky | *Complete forgetting, drifting away from awareness
exercise: «The old song fell into oblivion.» What does this mean? | The song was printed on the front page of a newspaper | *The song was completely forgotten | The song was sung every day in the school hall | The song was too loud to hear
exercise: Which word in this list is a noun? | quickly | happy | *oblivion | run
test: «The team was knocked into oblivion.» What does this suggest? | They went to sleep on the bus | They were given a prize for their hard work | They changed their kit | *Totally beaten and wiped out
test: «After a long day, he drifted into oblivion on the sofa.» What does oblivion mean? | Being very famous | *Deep sleep, not noticing anything around him | A loud argument with his sister | A trip to the shops
exam: Which meaning fits best in «the old song fell into oblivion»? | Very hungry | Giving out light | Dangerous | *Completely forgotten
